#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138064 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138455 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d85ef216-095d-589d-8d1d-5a1543d576a3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Terms of a Sequence Given as a List"}] :question/id #uuid "96401904-3d0f-56ac-ba31-eee3cbb016cb" :question/math-academy-id "e-1153" :question/problem "What is the sum of the first and last terms of the following sequence?\n\n$3,\\quad-5,\\quad7,\\quad-9,\\quad11,\\quad-13,\\quad15$" :question/worked-solution "The first term is $3$ and the last term is $15$. Their sum is\n\n$$\n3+15 = 18\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138065 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138457 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83b12b3a-2b6d-5fc4-80bb-c83415486b29" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Terms of a Sequence Using Sequence Notation"}] :question/id #uuid "e9ccec31-a359-57b6-9817-9460a5b53d51" :question/math-academy-id "e-1155" :question/problem "A sequence is defined as $a_n =n^2$ for $n\\geq 1$. What is the sum of its second and third terms?" :question/worked-solution "Using $a_n$ notation, the second and third terms are $a_2$ and $a_3$, respectively.\n\nWe can compute these values using the rule $a_n=n^2$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{2} &= 2^{2} = 4 \\\\\na_{3} &= 3^{2} = 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the second and third terms is\n\n$$\na_2+a_3=4+9=13\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138066 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138456 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c747ef7-8eec-5cbe-be45-98c4023db36c" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Terms of a Sequence Using Function Notation"}] :question/id #uuid "5060bada-52d2-5fef-b103-e375eb568c40" :question/math-academy-id "e-1156" :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the sequence $f(n) =5-2n$ for $n\\geq 1$?\n\n1. Its second term is larger than the first term\n2. Its third term is $-1$\n3. Its last term is $-5$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- The first and second terms of the sequence are represented by $f(1)$ and $f(2)$, respectively. We can compute these values using the rule $f(n) = 5-2n$, as follows;\n$f(1)|= 5 - 2(1) = 3; f(2)|= 5 - 2(2) = 1$\nWe see that the second term $f(2)=1$ is smaller than the first term $f(1)=3$. Therefore, statement I is false.\n- We compute the third term as follows:\n$f(3)=5-2(3)=-1$\nTherefore, statement II is true.\n- The sequence is defined for $n\\geq 1$, which means that there is no limit to how large $n$ can be. There is no last term, because the sequence keeps going on forever. Therefore, statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true."}]}
